<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!-- Every macOS app extension is sandboxed, and unlike on iOS that is a key
	     the target has to state rather than a property of being an extension.
	     Runner/DebugProfile.entitlements and Runner/Release.entitlements say the
	     same thing for the app. -->
	<key>com.apple.security.app-sandbox</key>
	<true/>

	<!-- The App Group is deliberately NOT here yet, and this is the note that
	     says so rather than an omission anyone has to rediscover.

	     What belongs here, once there is signing material:

	         <key>com.apple.security.application-groups</key>
	         <array>
	           <string>$(TeamIdentifierPrefix)group.com.bostrot.weft</string>
	         </array>

	     Three facts, in the order they matter.

	     1. The spelling differs from the iOS entitlements file, and not
	        cosmetically: App Groups on macOS are team-prefixed and iOS's are
	        not, so the same group is `group.com.bostrot.weft` there and
	        `<TeamID>.group.com.bostrot.weft` here. `$(TeamIdentifierPrefix)` is
	        the build setting that expands to `<TeamID>.`, which keeps the team id
	        itself out of version control. ../Shared/SnapshotContainer.swift
	        already records this and points at
	        docs/decisions/adr-002-ios-signing-handoff.md.

	     2. **Adding it now would break the build for everyone**, which is the
	        reason it is a comment rather than a key. Unlike the sandbox, an App
	        Group entitlement cannot be ad-hoc signed: with it present
	        `xcodebuild` refuses before it compiles anything —

	            error: "WeftWidget" requires a provisioning profile. Enable
	            development signing and select a provisioning profile in the
	            Signing & Capabilities editor.

	        — and because Runner depends on this target, that is `flutter build
	        macos` failing on any machine without the team's certificate. The iOS
	        half has no such problem: `flutter build ios --no-codesign` skips
	        signing entirely, so ios/WeftWidget/WeftWidget.entitlements carries
	        its group for real.

	     3. **It buys nothing until macos/Runner has the group too.** That target
	        does not request one either — Phase 04 left it out on the same
	        reasoning — so the writing side is equally inert:
	        `containerURL(forSecurityApplicationGroupIdentifier:)` returns nil in
	        both processes, the app reports the snapshot write as refused, and
	        this extension draws its signed-out state. Both are states the code
	        already survives, which is what makes authoring the target now and
	        provisioning it later the honest order — the phase's own rule, "Xcode
	        targets are authored but not signed". -->
</dict>
</plist>
